[1]   | #command  | Dialogue command: {write dialogue}                                                                                                                                                              | 
[2]   | #xcommand | Ex-dialogue command: {write dialogue and change image}                                                                                                                                          | 
[3]   | #token    | Dialogue token code: {$standard}                                                                                                                                                                | 
[4]   | #define   | {*} means {}.                                                                                                                                                                                   | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[5]   | *         | One day, a robin alighted upon a branch and sang sweetly with its clear, melodious voice.                                                                                                       | 
      |           | And then, a sparrow, drawn by the sweet melody, flew from somewhere, following the sound.                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[6]   | Sparrow   | "I wonder, could any of our kind possibly sing with such a beautiful voice?"                                                                                                                    | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[7]   | *         | And so, the sparrow wondered in amazement.                                                                                                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[8]   | *         | The sparrow quickly alighted upon the very branch where the robin was perched, singing its sweet song.                                                                                          | 
      |           | And when the sparrow looked closely at the singing bird, it found that, in appearance, size, and even in the color of its feathers,                                                             | 
      |           | it was not so different from itself after all.                                                                                                                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[9]   | *         | Upon reflection, the sparrow could not help but feel deeply dissatisfied.                                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[10]  | *         | Why is it, I wonder, that even from the moment we were born, we cannot produce such a beautiful song?                                                                                           | 
      |           | Though we have the same wings, the same beak, and two feet, why is it that only our voices differ so?                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[11]  | *         | If only we could sing such a beautiful voice, surely we would be cherished by humans as well," the sparrow thought.                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[12]  | *         | The sparrow harbored such discontent in its heart, but for a while, it remained silent, listening intently to the robin’s fervent song.                                                        | 
      |           | Then, as if drawn by the sound of the robin’s song, a crow appeared from somewhere, flying in and perching upon a branch near the tree.                                                        | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[13]  | *         | When the robin heard the strong beating of wings as the crow approached, it seemed startled, yet continued to sing as if nothing had happened.                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[14]  | *         | Seeing how the robin--so little different in appearance from themselves--was admired by all, the sparrow felt all the more burdened by its growing discontent.                                  | 
      |           | At last, the sparrow turned to the robin and asked.                                                                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[15]  | Sparrow   | "Dear Robin, why is it that you possess such a beautiful voice? Please, tell me the reason."                                                                                                    | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[16]  | Sparrow   | "I am a bird as well, and I do not believe I am so very different from you. So please, tell me--who taught you to sing with such a beautiful voice?                                             | 
      |           | I would very much like to go and learn from them too."                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[17]  | *         | …the sparrow said.                                                                                                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[18]  | *         | At that moment, the robin stopped singing for the first time. Turning to the sparrow, it said:                                                                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[19]  | Robin     | "Dear Sparrow, I understand your doubt--it is not unreasonable. But there is a story behind this.                                                                                               | 
      |           | Do you know of the time when the great sun had fallen deep into a valley and could not rise again?"                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[20]  | Robin     | "Our ancestors, along with the ancestors of the crow who now perches nearby, worked together to bind the sun with ropes and pull it back into the sky."                                         | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[21]  | Robin     | "To encourage the others, our forebears played flutes and reed pipes, and they sang songs to lift everyone’s spirits."                                                                         | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[22]  | Robin     | "And so, from that time on, their descendants--including myself--were blessed with these beautiful voices.                                                                                      | 
      |           | But your ancestors, I’m afraid, were flitting about the fields and meadows, offering no help in that time of need. And so it is that you and yours have led plain, ordinary lives ever since." | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[23]  | *         | Thus spoke the robin.                                                                                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[24]  | *         | The sparrow, who had listened in silence, tilted its head and said:                                                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[25]  | Sparrow   | "Is that truly so? How deeply shameful it is to hear. If that is indeed what happened, then I shall go to where the sun resides and offer my sincerest apology.                                 | 
      |           | Surely, then, the sun will forgive the negligence of our ancestors."                                                                                                                            | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[26]  | Sparrow   | "And in doing so, I shall be granted beautiful wings, and a voice as lovely as yours."                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[27]  | *         | So spoke the honest young sparrow, his heart filled with earnest resolve.                                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[28]  | Robin     | "That is no simple matter. Look to where the sun shines--see how swiftly the clouds rush by?                                                                                                    | 
      |           | It is because great winds are always blowing there."                                                                                                                                            | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[29]  | Robin     | "Surely, you would be swept away by those winds. Before anything else, you must find a way to cut through them."                                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[30]  | *         | Thus spoke the robin.                                                                                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[31]  | *         | The sparrow lifted its gaze to the vast sky above.                                                                                                                                              | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[32]  | Sparrow   | "Indeed, the clouds are racing. Just as you said, it seems a strong wind is blowing."                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[33]  | Sparrow   | "How can my small body rise higher and higher without being blown away by the wind?                                                                                                             | 
      |           | Won’t you please tell me?"                                                                                                                                                                     | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[34]  | Robin     | "If you truly feel that strongly, then I shall teach you."                                                                                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[35]  | Robin     | "For the next three years, you must train by flying over the rough seas, letting the wind beat against you.                                                                                     | 
      |           | And once you have mastered that, you shall take flight toward the place where the sun resides."                                                                                                 | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[36]  | *         | The sparrow listened with admiration to the words of the beautiful robin.                                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[37]  | *         | The crow, who had listened in silence to the tale, let out a caw and flew off to somewhere far away.                                                                                            | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[38]  | *         | Then the robin looked down at the sparrow and said softly,                                                                                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[39]  | Robin     | "Until we meet again."                                                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[40]  | *         | With those parting words, it flew off in the opposite direction from the crow.                                                                                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[41]  | *         | Left alone upon the branch, the sparrow made a solemn decision.                                                                                                                                 | 
      |           | And not long after, it too vanished--flying northward, its tiny form fading into the sky.                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[42]  | *         | One day, the sparrow flew among the swallows, soaring over the sea, gazing down at the white waves crashing against the rocks.                                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[43]  | *         | At another time, the sparrow joined the egrets, learning to glide through the turbulent air on windy days when the sea roared below and dark clouds swirled on every side.                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[44]  | *         | Through spring, summer, autumn, and winter, for three long years, the sorrowful sparrow lived upon the sea, mingling with egrets, swallows,                                                     | 
      |           | and various birds that migrated from the cold northern lands.                                                                                                                                   | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[45]  | *         | During that time, there were also days when the sky cleared to a soft green, and beneath it, the vast sea,                                                                                      | 
      |           | with its waves gently rolling onto the shore as though it were peacefully sleeping, weary from the weight of the world.                                                                         | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[46]  | *         | Such beautiful scenes were sights the sparrow had never been able to see while flying over the fields, forests, or rice paddies in its earlier days.                                            | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[47]  | *         | And on summer evenings, there were times when the sun, glowing a deep red, would silently sink into the sea like a great ball of fire rolling beneath the horizon.                              | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[48]  | *         | At that moment, the little sparrow remembered how, long ago, the sun had been bound with ropes, and how the crow, the robin,                                                                    | 
      |           | and various other birds had pulled it up from the deep, shadowy valley, lifting it back into the sky.                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[49]  | *         | Then, as though the sweet melody of the robin’s song echoed in the sparrow’s ears, there arose a nostalgic feeling, as if the sounds of flutes, drums,                                        | 
      |           | and the soft hum of the sho emerged from the beautiful, multicolored evening clouds, drifting over the sea.                                                                                     | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[50]  | Sparrow   | "It seems as though the sun is falling once again into that deep, dark valley,                                                                                                                  | 
      |           | Why is it, then, that no one pulls it up as they did in the past, yet it rises on its own every morning? This mystery fills me with wonder."                                                    | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[51]  | *         | --Thought the sparrow.                                                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[52]  | *         | And when the day finally arrived for the sparrow to fly toward the sun, it wondered what would become of it when the darkness fell,                                                             | 
      |           | the sun sinking into the valley once more. When night came, and the light of the stars began to shine in the deep, pale blue sky, where would it rest?                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[53]  | *         | Until the sparrow had heard the answer from the robin, it could not continue its long, arduous journey with peace of mind.                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[54]  | *         | In that time, the wind may blow, and the rain may fall.                                                                                                                                         | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[55]  | *         | The sparrow decided once again that it must meet the robin and ask about this matter.                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[56]  | *         | One day, the sparrow bade farewell to the elderly egret with whom it had been flying playfully over the waves, and flew towards the meadow where it had met the robin three years ago.          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[57]  | Sparrow   | "If I search for two or three days, surely I will come across that robin."                                                                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[58]  | *         | --The sparrow thought.                                                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[59]  | *         | The sparrow perched on tree branches, listening intently, hoping to hear the familiar song of the robin drifting from somewhere.                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[60]  | *         | And so, the sparrow made its way from one grove to another, wandering through the trees.                                                                                                        | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[61]  | *         | Just then, the sparrow happened to encounter the same crow it had once met before.                                                                                                              | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[62]  | Sparrow   | "Mr. Crow, Mr. Crow! What a fortunate moment to meet you. I’m so glad to see you well."                                                                                                        | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[63]  | *         | --Called the sparrow.                                                                                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[64]  | *         | The crow tilted his head and gazed intently at the sparrow.                                                                                                                                     | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[65]  | Crow      | "Ah, so it was you--the sparrow from before. You've changed so much, I hardly recognized you. Your wings have turned quite red."                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[66]  | *         | --The crow said.                                                                                                                                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[67]  | *         | The sparrow, startled,                                                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[68]  | Sparrow   | "You say… I’ve turned red?"                                                                                                                                                                   | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[69]  | *         | --Looked around at itself and asked.                                                                                                                                                            | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[70]  | Crow      | "You mean you haven't noticed?"                                                                                                                                                                 | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[71]  | *         | the crow said with a chuckle.                                                                                                                                                                   | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[72]  | Sparrow   | "I see now… my appearance has changed."                                                                                                                                                        | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[73]  | Crow      | "It seems you’ve flown so much under the sun… that you’ve become sunburned."                                                                                                                 | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[74]  | *         | --The crow said.                                                                                                                                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[75]  | *         | The sparrow suddenly let out a sorrowful cry and,                                                                                                                                               | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[76]  | Sparrow   | "I wish to fly swiftly toward the sun. I want to be of help, to become a splendid bird. That’s why I’ve been searching for the robin I once met."                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[77]  | *         | --The sparow replied.                                                                                                                                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[78]  | *         | Then, the crow laughed again, cawing loudly.                                                                                                                                                    | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[79]  | Crow      | "Did you really do what that robin told you to do? If so, then how unfortunate."                                                                                                                | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[80]  | Crow      | "The reason the robin said those meaningless things back then, was because it feared me and flattered me, making up such nonsense."                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[81]  | Crow      | "I’ve always known that robin is a talkative one, I thought I’d have some fun and tease it a little.                                                                                          | 
      |           | Why would my ancestors have pulled the sun up with a rope? Honestly, that robin is a master at telling lies."                                                                                   | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[82]  | Crow      | "You’ve believed that all this time?"                                                                                                                                                          | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[83]  | *         | The crow asked, with a look of astonishment on its face.                                                                                                                                        | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[84]  | *         | The sparrow was shocked twice.                                                                                                                                                                  | 
      |           | Then, it deeply mourned the fact that all its hard work over the long three years had been in vain.                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[85]  | Sparrow   | "Crow, I’ve spent three years training to fly up into the sky, and now, I’ve undergone training that makes me unafraid of rain or wind."                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[86]  | Sparrow   | "But has all of that become useless now?"                                                                                                                                                       | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[87]  | *         | The sparrow said, as if on the verge of tears.                                                                                                                                                  | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[88]  | Crow      | "No bird can ever rise up to where the sun shines."                                                                                                                                             | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[89]  | Crow      | "But, sparrow, now that you have become like this, you can never return to your homeland. No one will consider you one of their own."                                                           | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[90]  | *         | The crow said this with a tone full of pity.                                                                                                                                                    | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[91]  | *         | The red sparrow remained silent, lost in thought for a while, but eventually, instead of returning to its southern homeland, it flew off towards the north.                                     | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 
[92]  | *         | The sparrow returned to where the white herons and rock swallows were, heading towards the blue, blue sea.                                                                                      | 
      |           |                                                                                                                                                                                                 | 